# Provider API keys for the agent-hands scripts.
#
# Copy this file to the gitignored .env.local at the repo root and paste your own values in.
# This file holds NO values and is committed on purpose, so a fresh clone can see which keys
# exist without ever seeing one.
#
# No AI tool may read or edit the real file. The deny rules in .claude/settings.json and the
# PreToolUse guard in .claude/hooks/deny-env-access.py block any tool call whose file path or
# shell command names it. The reason is blunt: an agent that can read the key file can paste
# a key into a transcript, a commit or a bug report, and a leaked provider key stays billable
# to you until you notice it. The scripts load the keys inside their own process and print
# only results, so agents call scripts/x-fetch and scripts/li-fetch and never the file.
#
# Both scripts read the environment FIRST and fall back to the local file, so exporting a key
# in your shell or pulling it from a secrets manager works and is the better habit on any
# machine you share.
#
# A missing key is a loud failure in both scripts, never a silent empty result. A tool that
# answers "no posts found" when what it actually has is no credentials is how a brief goes
# out empty and confident.

# scripts/x-fetch primary provider. Public X profile plus recent posts, fetched provider-side
# so your own X account is never the actor.
TWITTERAPI_IO_API_KEY=

# scripts/x-fetch fallback provider, and the only provider scripts/li-fetch uses (public
# LinkedIn profile scrape, also provider-side, no cookies and no session). Deliberately a
# different vendor on a different pipe: the real risk with a cheap unofficial gateway is
# vendor continuity, not account safety, so the failover is automatic rather than a manual
# swap you discover you need while a brief is due.
BRIGHTDATA_API_KEY=
